WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:10.880
Okay, so we typically use the subqueries in the From clause in order to create temporary results sets

00:10.880 --> 00:14.560
that act as a table for the main query.

00:14.560 --> 00:19.240
So it's like in some scenarios we cannot use the tables directly from the database.

00:19.240 --> 00:23.440
We have to prepare it somehow before we do our actual query.

00:23.480 --> 00:23.720
Okay.

00:23.760 --> 00:27.720
So let's check the syntax of the subquery inside the From clause.

00:27.720 --> 00:33.000
So we start with the usual stuff where we go and say select and few columns that we want to retrieve.

00:33.040 --> 00:34.920
And then we say okay from.

00:34.960 --> 00:39.800
Usually after the from comes the table name from our database that we want to query.

00:39.800 --> 00:45.280
But this time instead of writing the table name, we're going to have another SQL query.

00:45.280 --> 00:47.680
So that means we don't define the table name.

00:47.680 --> 00:51.120
We define another Select statements where we have as well.

00:51.160 --> 00:54.480
Again select a column from specific table.

00:54.480 --> 00:56.040
And then maybe we have a filter.

00:56.040 --> 01:02.550
And in order now to tell SQL this is a subquery we have to use the parentheses so we can have the parentheses

01:02.550 --> 01:04.190
at the start and at the ends.

01:04.230 --> 01:05.430
This is a subquery.

01:05.430 --> 01:07.310
This is not the main query.

01:07.310 --> 01:12.790
And after the parentheses we can go and define the alias for the results that we're going to get from

01:12.790 --> 01:13.790
this subquery.

01:13.790 --> 01:16.590
In many databases this alias is an optional.

01:16.590 --> 01:19.950
But for the SQL server we have to go and specify an alias.

01:19.950 --> 01:22.190
So it is a must in SQL server.

01:22.190 --> 01:24.670
So again we call this a subquery.

01:24.910 --> 01:28.270
And the outer query we call it a main query.

01:28.270 --> 01:31.710
So this is the syntax of the subquery in the From clause okay.

01:31.750 --> 01:32.910
So now we have the following task.

01:32.910 --> 01:39.590
And it says find the products that have a price higher than the average price of all products.

01:39.590 --> 01:41.070
So we're going to do it step by step.

01:41.070 --> 01:42.590
And here we have two steps.

01:42.590 --> 01:46.910
The first one is that we have to go and calculate the average price of all products.

01:46.910 --> 01:52.150
And the second step we're going to use this value in order to filter the table products in order to

01:52.190 --> 01:56.310
find the prices that is higher than this average price.

01:56.310 --> 02:00.890
So let's start with the first step where we're going to find the average price, I'm going to select

02:00.890 --> 02:02.130
the following information.

02:02.130 --> 02:09.450
So product ID price from the table sales products.

02:10.010 --> 02:11.730
So let's go and execute it.

02:11.730 --> 02:14.490
So now we have the products and as well the prices.

02:14.490 --> 02:19.010
And we need this price here in order to compare it with the average price.

02:19.010 --> 02:20.890
So that means we need this price.

02:20.890 --> 02:24.370
And as well side by side we need the average price.

02:24.370 --> 02:26.570
So that means we need aggregations and details.

02:26.570 --> 02:29.730
And that's why we're going to go with the window function average.

02:29.730 --> 02:30.970
So let's go and do this.

02:31.010 --> 02:32.210
This is very simple.

02:32.530 --> 02:34.890
So it's going to be the average price.

02:35.730 --> 02:37.850
And we don't want to partition the data.

02:37.850 --> 02:39.850
So it's going to be an over empty.

02:39.850 --> 02:42.850
And this is going to be the average price like this.

02:42.850 --> 02:44.410
So let's go and execute it.

02:44.410 --> 02:46.890
And with that we have calculated the average price.

02:46.890 --> 02:48.610
So now we have all the informations.

02:48.610 --> 02:50.850
In the first step we have the average price.

02:50.850 --> 02:53.130
We have the price and as well the products.

02:53.130 --> 02:59.240
So now the next step is that we have to go and filter the data to find out all the products where the

02:59.240 --> 03:01.480
price is higher than the average.

03:01.680 --> 03:06.200
That means we will do this step based on those informations that we have now.

03:06.200 --> 03:10.440
So that means we have to go and use the logic of subquery and main query.

03:10.520 --> 03:15.920
Since this is the first step to prepare the data, we are going to use this as a subquery.

03:15.920 --> 03:20.160
So we're going to call this a subquery like this.

03:20.640 --> 03:23.440
And we have to go and use it in the main query.

03:23.440 --> 03:24.640
So how are we going to do that.

03:24.640 --> 03:27.240
We have to go and write the main query.

03:27.240 --> 03:30.720
So it's going to be I'm going to start over here select.

03:30.720 --> 03:34.000
And then I will take all the columns from.

03:34.160 --> 03:37.120
So this is the main query.

03:37.160 --> 03:39.600
Let me just make this a little bit smaller.

03:39.760 --> 03:41.440
And what we're going to do now.

03:41.440 --> 03:44.800
So now the main query is going to get the data from the subquery.

03:44.960 --> 03:49.080
So the whole thing is going to be used inside the From clause.

03:49.080 --> 03:54.600
So now in order to put the subquery inside the main query we have to go and use the parenthesis.

03:54.600 --> 03:57.560
So we're going to have it at the start and as well at the end.

03:57.670 --> 04:04.070
And what we usually do, we go and add like a tab in order to understand okay, this is the subquery.

04:04.310 --> 04:06.230
And then this is the main query.

04:06.230 --> 04:10.870
So now one more thing that we have to add for the whole subquery in the SQL server.

04:10.910 --> 04:12.670
Thus we have to give it an alias.

04:12.670 --> 04:14.950
So you can go and give it any name that you would like.

04:14.990 --> 04:18.790
I usually go with only one character with the T it stands for table.

04:18.790 --> 04:20.270
So you can use anything that you want.

04:20.310 --> 04:24.790
But we have in SQL server to give an alias for the subquery.

04:24.790 --> 04:28.590
So now what we are saying we are saying select everything from the subquery.

04:28.590 --> 04:32.510
If you go over here and execute it you will get the exact same results.

04:32.510 --> 04:34.670
Because the main query is doing nothing.

04:34.670 --> 04:37.630
It's saying just select everything from the subquery.

04:37.630 --> 04:40.910
But now in order to solve the task, we are not interested with all products.

04:40.910 --> 04:44.590
We are interested only the products where the price is higher than the average.

04:44.830 --> 04:48.030
That's why we have to go and use the Where clause.

04:48.510 --> 04:54.910
So we're going to say where the price is higher than the average price.

04:55.070 --> 04:58.130
So this filtering is done in the main query.

04:58.130 --> 05:00.450
It's not inside the subquery.

05:00.450 --> 05:03.090
So now that means in the main query we are doing something.

05:03.130 --> 05:04.730
Let's go and execute it.

05:04.730 --> 05:10.170
And with that we solve the task we are getting now two products where the price is higher than the average

05:10.170 --> 05:10.770
price.

05:10.770 --> 05:12.010
So as you can see it's very simple.

05:12.010 --> 05:18.130
If the task has multiple steps then we can do that using multiple sub queries until we have the main

05:18.130 --> 05:18.610
query.

05:18.610 --> 05:19.890
And we can learn from this.

05:19.890 --> 05:24.450
That the subquery is here is only to support the main query.

05:24.450 --> 05:29.610
So we are preparing here that all the data that we need in order to have the final result for the main

05:29.610 --> 05:30.130
query.

05:30.130 --> 05:35.170
So for this task we cannot go immediately calculating the results we have first.

05:35.170 --> 05:39.930
So for this kind of task we cannot immediately like put everything in one select query.

05:39.970 --> 05:45.890
We have first to prepare the data in one sub query and then pass the values for the main query.

05:45.890 --> 05:49.250
And this is what we mean with the table sub query.

05:49.250 --> 05:53.690
And here one quick tip for you if you would like to see the intermediate results that we are getting

05:53.690 --> 05:59.520
from the subquery, you can go and highlight the subquery itself without the parenthesis.

05:59.520 --> 06:02.320
So we are just highlighting the subquery.

06:02.360 --> 06:04.320
You can go now and execute it.

06:04.320 --> 06:07.440
And with that SQL will not go and execute everything.

06:07.720 --> 06:10.320
SQL can execute only what you are highlighting.

06:10.320 --> 06:14.480
So this is a really nice way in order to see the results of the subquery.

06:14.480 --> 06:19.480
As you are like debugging or searching for errors, you can go and see the intermediate results that

06:19.480 --> 06:21.400
is used from the main query.

06:21.440 --> 06:27.600
And of course if you deselect and not highlight anything and execute SQL, you can go and execute everything,

06:27.600 --> 06:28.600
the whole query.

06:28.600 --> 06:33.480
So this is how we use the table subquery inside the From clause.

06:33.520 --> 06:33.840
All right.

06:33.840 --> 06:34.920
So let's have another task.

06:34.960 --> 06:39.920
And it says rank the customers based on their total amount of sales.

06:40.040 --> 06:42.680
So again if you check here we have like two steps.

06:42.680 --> 06:45.720
First we have to find the total amount of sales.

06:45.960 --> 06:49.880
And then after that we have to go and rank the customers.

06:49.880 --> 06:51.320
So again we have like two steps.

06:51.320 --> 06:53.960
And we can use the subqueries in order to solve it.

06:53.960 --> 06:57.820
So let's start with the first step where we're going to find the total amount of sales.

06:58.020 --> 07:06.460
So let's go and select the customer ID and as well the sales from the table sales orders.

07:08.740 --> 07:10.020
Let's go and execute it.

07:10.020 --> 07:14.900
So now in the output we have like multiple customers and their sales we have to go and now find the

07:14.900 --> 07:17.260
total amount of sales for each customer.

07:17.300 --> 07:19.620
That means we have to go and use the groupby.

07:19.900 --> 07:23.220
So we're going to go and summarize the sales.

07:24.340 --> 07:30.220
So total sales and then group up the data by the customer ID.

07:31.140 --> 07:32.460
So like this.

07:32.460 --> 07:33.860
Let's go and execute it.

07:33.900 --> 07:36.260
Now as you can see in the output we have four customers.

07:36.260 --> 07:38.580
And we have the total sales for each customer.

07:38.580 --> 07:40.860
And with that we have solved the first step.

07:40.860 --> 07:43.180
We have the total amount of sales for each customer.

07:43.380 --> 07:49.060
And we have now prepared the data for the next query in order to rank the customers.

07:49.100 --> 07:55.290
So now I think you're already getting how important are the subqueries in order to do step by step analyses.

07:55.290 --> 07:57.610
So this is our subquery.

07:57.930 --> 07:59.610
Now we need the min query.

07:59.610 --> 08:01.770
So I will start preparing it.

08:01.770 --> 08:03.570
So min query like this.

08:03.610 --> 08:06.370
And let's go first and select everything.

08:06.370 --> 08:08.730
So select star from.

08:08.770 --> 08:12.170
Let me just make this a little bit bigger like this.

08:12.210 --> 08:15.210
And now we have to go and convert this query to a subquery.

08:15.210 --> 08:16.730
So we need the parenthesis.

08:16.890 --> 08:19.370
So the starting and the ending.

08:19.610 --> 08:22.050
And for the SQL server I'm going to give it an alias.

08:22.050 --> 08:24.850
And I would like to push everything to the right side.

08:24.850 --> 08:26.850
So let's go and execute it.

08:26.890 --> 08:27.370
Perfect.

08:27.370 --> 08:29.210
So it is working with that.

08:29.210 --> 08:33.850
The subquery is passing the data in the from clause to the main query.

08:33.890 --> 08:35.970
Now of course the main query is now is useless.

08:36.010 --> 08:37.810
It's just like selecting the data.

08:37.810 --> 08:40.010
We have to go and calculate the rank.

08:40.050 --> 08:43.090
And for that we have a very nice window function.

08:43.090 --> 08:45.170
So we're going to go and use the rank.

08:45.170 --> 08:47.770
So it doesn't need any parameters over.

08:47.810 --> 08:50.490
We have to sort the data order by.

08:50.530 --> 08:56.040
So we have to go and sort the data by the total sales descending from the highest to the lowest.

08:56.040 --> 08:59.680
So we're going to go with the total sales and descending.

08:59.680 --> 09:05.200
So now as you can see, we are using the total sales that we have already prepared in the subquery.

09:05.200 --> 09:10.560
So without preparing first the data we will not be able to rank the customers in the main query.

09:10.560 --> 09:11.680
So that's it.

09:11.680 --> 09:13.080
Let's go and execute it.

09:13.120 --> 09:15.040
And with that SQL sorted our data.

09:15.040 --> 09:20.400
And we have a nice ranking based on the data that we had from the subquery.

09:20.400 --> 09:25.400
So this is the highest customer with the sales and then the customer number one and so on.

09:25.400 --> 09:27.640
So again in this task we have like multiple steps.

09:27.640 --> 09:32.080
And we use the power of the subqueries in order to do it step by step.

09:32.080 --> 09:36.160
So that's all on how to use the subquery inside the From clause okay.

09:36.200 --> 09:39.040
So now let's see quickly how SQL executed our query.

09:39.040 --> 09:42.240
So we have here our query and we are querying the table orders.

09:42.240 --> 09:46.040
So the first step does SQL going to go and identify the subquery.

09:46.280 --> 09:48.320
And then it's going to go and execute it.

09:48.320 --> 09:53.700
So SQL is going to go and execute the subquery parts where we are aggregating the data based on the

09:53.700 --> 09:54.580
customer ID.

09:54.580 --> 10:00.340
So once the subquery is executed, the next step is that the result is going to be introduced as an

10:00.380 --> 10:01.740
intermediate results.

10:01.820 --> 10:04.500
So these results we will not see it in the output.

10:04.540 --> 10:07.620
It's going to be like temporarily saved in the memory.

10:07.620 --> 10:11.180
So now the next step is that SQL is going to go to the main query.

10:11.180 --> 10:14.540
And it's going to execute it based on the intermediate results.

10:14.540 --> 10:18.700
So that means the main query will not go back to the original table.

10:18.740 --> 10:21.380
It's going to go and query the intermediate results.

10:21.380 --> 10:22.660
So here what SQL is going to do.

10:22.700 --> 10:27.900
I'm going to go and rank the intermediate results by introducing a new column where we see the ranks

10:27.900 --> 10:28.940
123, four.

10:28.980 --> 10:31.980
And the output of the main query is going to be the final results.

10:31.980 --> 10:33.140
So as you can see it's very simple.

10:33.180 --> 10:35.700
First SQL is executing the subquery.

10:35.900 --> 10:39.340
And the result of the subquery is going to be used in the main query.

10:39.340 --> 10:43.420
And once the main query is executed we will get the final results.

10:43.420 --> 10:46.980
So the subquery here is only supporting the main query.

10:46.980 --> 10:52.500
So those are the steps that SQL uses in order to execute the subqueries.
